WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.120
- Ah, crap!

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.140
- [Narrator] This is what
that scene looked like

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.086
behind the scenes.

00:00:07.086 --> 00:00:08.090
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:10.040
And that's not a stunt double.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.190
Tom Holland got hit by
this car replica 17 times,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.310
then had to lunge himself forward

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:19.440
to remain horizontal for
the rest of the stunt.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.550
And it's not the first time
Tom has done his own stunts.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.500
As a trained acrobat and gymnast,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.840
he's dropped hundreds of feet in the air

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.770
and has backflipped off a replica

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.770
of the Washington Monument.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:34.636
Here are five dangerous
stunts Tom performed himself.

00:00:34.636 --> 00:00:35.490
(Tom grunts)

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:36.323
- So fun.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.080
- [Narrator] For 2019's
"Spider-Man: Far from Home,"

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.600
Tom trained with stunt
coordinator George Cottle

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.507
for five weeks to prepare
for all of the film's stunts.

00:00:46.507 --> 00:00:49.870
But the most intense was
this web-swinging sequence

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.810
with MJ, played by Zendaya.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.070
Spider-Man's iconic skyscraper swings

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.370
have been a consistent
challenge in the franchise,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.630
predominately created using
CGI in the earlier films.

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.190
But for Tom, the filmmakers leaned

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.930
into practical stunts as much as possible.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.690
Tom had a bodycam attached to him,

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.730
and both actors were raised by a crane

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.053
with a wire rig to over 100 feet.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.600
The two were then dropped
and swung in an arc,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.710
with cardboard boxes placed below them

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.083
as a safety precaution.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.810
To capture the landing and takeoff,

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.700
they were lifted BTS by
cranes to different heights

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.190
outside of Grand Central Station
and Madison Square Garden.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.970
And for close-up shots of them in midair,

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.370
a large blue screen and
wind fans were set up

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.460
in the parking lot of a Brooklyn school,

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.800
the interior of which was used

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.770
for Peter Parker's high school.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.880
According to Zendaya, the
shot from 100-foot-high swing

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.020
wasn't included in the final cut,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.170
but the final shot combined
in-camera practical stunts

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.040
with digidoubles for seamless movements

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.983
like never before in the series.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.100
You might think the
Washington Monument scene

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.800
in 2017's Spider-Man:
Homecoming was all CGI,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.750
but that was really Tom
climbing and leaping around

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.280
to save his friends.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.730
Various sections of the
monument were replicated

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.080
and built on a soundstage in Atlanta.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.330
Specifically, the side
of the monument was built

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.570
at an angle for the initial ascent.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.830
Then wires assisted Tom
in the practical climb.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.060
With clever camera angles,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.770
the filmmakers created the illusion

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:16.793
that the building was actually vertical.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.220
Tom then had to perform various
jumps off another replica

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.780
that represented the very top

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.010
and the sides of the building.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.730
Here he is doing a backflip
off the top into the air,

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.620
assisted with cables.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.390
And in this shot he's doing a backflip

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.210
into what's known in the
stunt world as a face-off,

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.030
which prevents injury upon impact

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:39.270
by landing flat on his back.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:40.780
For stunts that were impossible

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.410
or too dangerous to perform,

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.230
like swinging from the helicopter,

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.010
the filmmakers used CG.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.570
But the movements were based
on motion-captured footage

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.880
of Tom's actual stunt work.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.340
This made the final CG shots
appear even more realistic

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.303
and in Tom's signature style.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.750
Later in the same Monument
scene of Homecoming,

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.220
Spider-Man is pulled headfirst
into an elevator shaft.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:07.530
Tom considered this scene

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.870
one of his wildest stunts for Homecoming.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.920
It required him to have his
legs in a split position,

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.520
and then nose dive off a
ledge, suspended with wires.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.320
Tom was initially hesitant
about doing this himself,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.670
according to his stunt
double Chris Silcox.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.490
On top of his nerves, Tom
had to wear a corset harness,

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:26.020
which was so tight he was worried

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.210
about throwing up in his mask.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.820
In the end, Tom decided
to attempt the stunt

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:32.964
without using the stunt double.

00:03:32.964 --> 00:03:35.297
(all cheer)

00:03:36.867 --> 00:03:38.570
"In Spider-Man: Far From Home,"

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:42.120
Tom wasn't dealing with just
heights, but water as well.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.330
He needed to navigate quickly
in and out of bot the canals

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.570
and boats during this
battle with the Elementals.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.680
The crew recreated Venice
as a soundstage near London

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.730
with exact replicas of the Rialto bridge.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.550
They also recreated restaurants
and blue-green canal water.

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.130
To pull off this sequence safely,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.790
Tom practiced leaping
across wider platforms

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.783
in a clip he shared on his Instagram.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:08.150
For the actual stunt, he
was attached to a wire

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.750
while he ran across a
row of poles in the canal

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.583
and finally pole-vaulted onto the bridge.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.290
Tom later said he regretted
not wearing shin guards

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.730
like the stunt coordinator suggested.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.530
The water was practically manipulated

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.100
to create massive waves slamming into Tom

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.300
as he fought the Elementals.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.350
At one point, he was rigged to a ratchet

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.060
and pulled backwards into
the wall of the bridge,

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.010
then soaked with thousands
of gallons of water.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.083
He had to perform this stunt twice.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.020
His stunt double Greg
Townley stood for a few takes

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.240
when the wave initially
struck Tom on the bridge,

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.660
which Tom poked fun at on his Instagram.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.993
(men shout)

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.193
- Greg?

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.780
Tom described this stunt
in 2022's "Uncharted"

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.670
as the most difficult of his
career, and for good reason.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:03.250
The scene required him
to jump through midair

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.340
from one set of flying cargo
containers to the next,

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:09.523
then get hit by a car
and ejected from a plane.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.270
The crew built a blue screen
set in the parking lot

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:16.330
of the studio to help
capture natural daylight.

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:20.350
This scene using crates was
shot in two separate sequences.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:23.080
For the first sequence, when
Tom falls out of the plane,

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.290
supervising stunt coordinator Scott Rogers

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.380
decided to stage the action vertically

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:30.160
to better simulate flying through the air.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.930
The special effects team
used giant robotic arms

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:35.770
to safely hold and move the boxes.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.420
And Tom was secured with wires

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.790
as he hung onto the crates
and flew through the air.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:41.850
For the second sequence,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.960
when Tom is climbing back into the plane

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.630
and jumping crate to crate,

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.660
the boxes were held more horizontally.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.260
And for this final collision with the car,

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.093
- Ah, crap!

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.350
- [Narrator] the team
built the front section

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:56.510
of a fake vehicle,

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.880
which was replaced by a
CGI car in the final cut.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.200
Tom and the fake car were
attached to the same rig,

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.830
with the actor wearing three sets of wires

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.190
to stabilize him.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.520
The rig pulled the fake hood towards Tom,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:08.910
knocking him backwards.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.220
He then needed to remain horizontal

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.060
while flying out of the plane.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:17.070
To get this shot, Tom had to
be fake hit around 17 times,

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:20.750
with this entire scene taking
several weeks to complete.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:21.750
The result?

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.540
A realistic stunt that
matches the excitement

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.483
from the popular video game
scene it was pulled from.

00:06:28.366 --> 00:06:31.344
(Nathan screams)

00:06:31.344 --> 00:06:34.011
(upbeat music)

